  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1115 AM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nISOLATED SHOWERS AND STORMS FRIDAY AND SATURDAY:\n\nA weak jet accompanied by some modest moisture will nose into the \nFour Corners region tonight. Some of the hi-res models support the \ndevelopment of showers along the moisture transport gradient, \nespecially in southern foothills of the San Juans. This moisture \nremains in place for much of Friday and Saturday, especially along \nthe Divide. As a result, afternoon convection favoring the higher \nterrain will return to the forecast, but most locales will remain \ndry with seasonable temperatures and breezy afternoon winds.\n\nMONSOONAL MOISTURE SURGES SUNDAY AND MONDAY: \n\nA strong low pressure over the Pacific Northwest will advect a plume \nof anamolous moisture on Sunday and Monday. This could result \nin scattered to widespread convection across the region. PWATs \nupwards of 200% of normal will support the potential for heavy \nrainfall and flash flooding, especially around recent burn scars\nand areas with steep terrain. This threat is particularly \nheightened where soils have reached peak saturation after \nmultiple rounds of storms this past week. Models are beginning \nto show that the peak moisture might be overnight Sunday. Also, \nattm on Sunday afternoon there is not much instability or it \nmay be limited to a certain area. This will play a role in the\ncoverage and intensity of showers and storms.\n\nBeyond Monday the low pressure moves across the northern US causing \nhigh pressure to build over the region. The uncertainty is if we \nstay in the moist southwest flow. There is also signs of an active \nPacific Northwest, which may too play a role in precipitation \nchances.",
